WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.170
How creams work, and a clean, safe workspace.

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.040
This is the video for module one of Natural Skincare.

3
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:20.700
If you make whipped shea butter or body oils, you make anhydrous products. Anhydrous means without water. A facial oil is like that. It needs no preservative, but the oils can go rancid.

4
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:34.410
Creams and lotions are mostly water, held together with oil. That smooth mixture is called an emulsion. The moment a product contains water, bacteria, yeast and mould can grow in it.

5
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.840
So a broad-spectrum preservative, one that works against all three, is not optional.

6
00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:49.370
A badly made butter usually just feels grainy. But an unpreserved cream can grow germs that customers cannot see or smell.

7
00:00:50.620 --> 00:01:01.000
And then they rub it onto their face. So treat every water-based product as one that must be made cleanly, preserved properly, and tested before anyone else uses it.

8
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:18.060
Every cream has the same building blocks. The water phase is the biggest part: distilled water, plus glycerin, which draws moisture to the skin. Never use tap, borehole, sachet or bottled water. Their minerals can shorten shelf life.

9
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:25.440
The oil phase holds your oils and butters. Light oils give a thin feel, butters make it richer.

10
00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:36.310
The emulsifying wax holds oil and water together. Ordinary beeswax is not an emulsifier. Beeswax and water recipes often separate.

11
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:43.810
The cool-down phase holds the preservative, which goes in after cooling, because heat can damage it.

12
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:59.990
For example, Amaka in Enugu finds a simple lotion formula on Humblebee and Me, a respected formulation blog. Distilled water 76.5 percent, vegetable glycerin 10 percent. That is the water phase.

13
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:08.410
Emulsifying Wax NF 3 percent, and fractionated coconut oil 10 percent. That is the oil phase.

14
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.270
And Liquid Germall Plus, a preservative, at 0.5 percent, in the cool-down.

15
00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.160
She adds them up, and they total 100.

16
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:36.200
Then she checks the essentials. A named emulsifier, and a named broad-spectrum preservative. If a recipe has water but no real emulsifier or no preservative, it is not ready to make for other people, however beautiful the video looks.

17
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:49.870
Cleaning removes dirt and grease. Sanitising kills most germs that remain. You need both, in that order, because dirt protects germs. Wash every tool in hot water with dish soap.

18
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.890
Dry them fully, not with a shared kitchen cloth.

19
00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.700
Wipe your work surface with soap and water, then sanitise it.

20
00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:19.520
Spray tools, jars, lids and the blender head with 70 percent isopropyl alcohol. 70 works better than 100, because its water helps kill germs. If you only find 99 percent, Bisi's method is 354 ml topped up with distilled water to 500 ml.

21
00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:24.050
Wash your hands, put on clean gloves and spray them too.

22
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:33.640
Isopropyl alcohol is highly flammable. Never spray it near a gas flame, and let it evaporate fully before you switch on heat.

23
00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:50.790
You will heat wax and water to about 75 degrees Celsius. That can cause serious burns. Use oven gloves, keep handles turned inward, keep children out of the kitchen, and never leave a pot on heat unattended, even if the power goes off.

24
00:03:52.040 --> 00:04:00.480
If hot wax or oil touches skin, cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes, and seek medical help for large or deep burns.

25
00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:07.790
Keep the blender cable and sockets away from the water bath, and unplug the blender before touching the blade.

26
00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:29.650
Before you turn on the heat, tick every item. Tools washed and dried. Surfaces and tools sanitised. Hair covered and gloves on. Formula printed. A scale that reads to 0.01 g, because 0.5 percent of a 200 g batch is just 1 g. And children and pets out.

27
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:40.240
To remember. Water in a product always means a broad-spectrum preservative. Beeswax is not an emulsifier. Use distilled water only.

28
00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:46.160
Clean first, then sanitise. And let alcohol dry before heat, and never leave the pot.

29
00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:55.780
Now sort some products at home into water-free and water-based, sort a formula into its three phases, and set up your clean production corner.

30
00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:58.070
Continue with the lessons below.
